# Producer-Consumer Using Pipe

## Title
Producer-Consumer Communication using Pipe and fork()

## Objective
To demonstrate inter-process communication (IPC) between a parent process and a child process using a pipe.

## Description
This program creates a pipe using the pipe() system call and then creates a child process using fork().

The parent process acts as the Producer.
The child process acts as the Consumer.

The Producer generates five values:
10, 20, 30, 40, and 50

These values are written into the pipe by the Producer.
The Consumer reads the values from the pipe and displays them.

## System Calls Used
- pipe() – Creates a communication channel between processes.
- fork() – Creates a child process.
- write() – Writes data into the pipe.
- read() – Reads data from the pipe.
- close() – Closes the unused/read/write end of the pipe.
- getpid() – Displays the process ID.

## Working
1. A pipe is created using pipe(fd).
2. fork() creates a child process.
3. The child process acts as the Consumer.
4. The Consumer closes the writing end of the pipe.
5. The Consumer reads five values from the pipe.
6. The parent process acts as the Producer.
7. The Producer closes the reading end of the pipe.
8. The Producer generates values from 10 to 50.
9. The Producer writes the values into the pipe.
10. The Consumer reads and displays the values.

## Compilation
gcc practical.c -o practical

## Execution
./practical

## Expected Output
Producer <PID>
Produced 10
Produced 20
Produced 30
Produced 40
Produced 50
Consumer <PID>
Consumed 10
Consumed 20
Consumed 30
Consumed 40
Consumed 50

## Conclusion
The program demonstrates inter-process communication between a parent and child process using a pipe, where the parent produces data and the child consumes the data.
